"The prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. Favorable findings include negative surgical margins and the absence of seminal vesicle invasion and lymph node metastasis, which are positive prognostic indicators. Conversely, the preoperative PSA of 6.2 and the pathological T3a stage contribute to increased risk. The biopsy-to-prostatectomy difference is noted as the Gleason pattern and ISUP report are missing, which widens uncertainty regarding the true tumor biology. The nodal status is documented as no lymph node metastasis, which is favorable. The primary countervailing factor is the preoperative PSA level and the T3a stage. Information that would significantly revise this prognosis includes the postoperative PSA and its subsequent trajectory, as this directly informs the risk of recurrence. The current uncertainty is partially due to the missing biopsy details. The fixed estimated horizon is 37.33 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 29.86–44.80 months; its coverage has not been validated."
